WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.141
Welcome to domain two of the Network Plus N10-009 exam: network implementation.

00:00:05.141 --> 00:00:07.678
It is worth twenty percent of the exam.

00:00:07.678 --> 00:00:13.990
We will cover routing, switching features, wireless, and the physical factors of an installation.

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:15.912
Start with routing.

00:00:15.912 --> 00:00:21.315
A static route is entered by hand, adds no overhead, and does not adapt to failures.

00:00:21.315 --> 00:00:24.660
Dynamic routing lets routers share routes and adapt.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:30.513
The default route, zero dot zero dot zero dot zero slash zero, is the route of last resort.

00:00:30.513 --> 00:00:39.390
When several routes match, the router picks the longest prefix match first, then the lowest administrative distance, then the best metric.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:45.179
Remember the distances: connected zero, static one, O S P F one-ten, and R I P one-twenty.

00:00:45.179 --> 00:00:52.190
N A T translates private addresses, and P A T lets many hosts share one public address by using port numbers.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:54.284
The routing protocols.

00:00:54.284 --> 00:00:59.352
R I P is a distance vector protocol that counts hops, with a maximum of fifteen.

00:00:59.352 --> 00:01:04.166
O S P F is a link-state protocol, an open standard that uses cost and areas.

00:01:04.166 --> 00:01:10.374
E I G R P is Cisco's advanced distance vector protocol, with an administrative distance of ninety.

00:01:10.374 --> 00:01:18.038
And B G P is a path vector protocol that runs between autonomous systems, which is how the internet is stitched together.

00:01:18.038 --> 00:01:25.007
Also know first hop redundancy: routers share a virtual I P address so the default gateway survives a failure.

00:01:25.007 --> 00:01:28.997
H S R P and G L B P are Cisco, and V R R P is an open standard.

00:01:29.697 --> 00:01:30.610
Now switching.

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:34.128
V LANs split a switch into separate broadcast domains.

00:01:34.128 --> 00:01:36.930
An access port carries one V LAN, untagged.

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:41.296
A trunk port carries many V LANs using eight oh two dot one Q tags.

00:01:41.296 --> 00:01:46.248
The native V LAN is the untagged V LAN on a trunk, and both ends must match.

00:01:46.248 --> 00:01:50.288
Traffic between V LANs needs a router or a layer three switch.

00:01:50.288 --> 00:01:53.742
A voice V LAN keeps phone traffic separate from data.

00:01:54.442 --> 00:01:57.543
Spanning tree blocks redundant links to prevent loops.

00:01:57.543 --> 00:02:01.105
The switch with the lowest bridge I D becomes the root bridge.

00:02:01.105 --> 00:02:06.619
Ports take roles: root ports face the root, designated ports forward, and alternate ports block.

00:02:06.619 --> 00:02:10.008
Rapid S T P converges much faster than the classic version.

00:02:10.008 --> 00:02:17.130
Link aggregation bundles several links into one logical link for bandwidth and redundancy, and L A C P is the open standard.

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:21.094
Spanning tree treats the bundle as one link, so it does not block it.

00:02:21.094 --> 00:02:23.966
Port mirroring copies traffic to an analyzer port.

00:02:24.666 --> 00:02:26.662
Know the common switch features.

00:02:26.662 --> 00:02:29.718
Port security limits the mac addresses on a port.

00:02:29.718 --> 00:02:32.588
D H C P snooping blocks rogue D H C P servers.

00:02:32.588 --> 00:02:39.387
B P D U guard shuts down a port that receives spanning tree messages, such as when someone plugs in a switch.

00:02:39.387 --> 00:02:47.683
Power over Ethernet comes in levels: eight oh two dot three A F delivers about fifteen watts, A T about thirty, and B T up to ninety.

00:02:47.683 --> 00:02:53.422
Jumbo frames raise the M T U to about nine thousand bytes, and are used for storage traffic.

00:02:53.422 --> 00:02:55.792
And Q o S prioritizes voice and video.

00:02:56.492 --> 00:02:57.065
Wireless.

00:02:57.065 --> 00:03:02.027
Eight oh two dot eleven N is Wi-Fi four, on two point four and five gigahertz.

00:03:02.027 --> 00:03:04.382
A C is Wi-Fi five, on five gigahertz.

00:03:04.382 --> 00:03:07.945
A X is Wi-Fi six, and six E adds the six gigahertz band.

00:03:07.945 --> 00:03:09.153
B E is Wi-Fi seven.

00:03:09.153 --> 00:03:17.552
Two point four gigahertz has the longest range but the most interference, and its non-overlapping channels are one, six, and eleven.

00:03:17.552 --> 00:03:22.197
Five gigahertz has more channels and less interference but shorter range.

00:03:22.897 --> 00:03:30.306
For wireless security, W P A two uses A E S, and W P A three uses S A E, which resists offline password guessing.

00:03:30.306 --> 00:03:34.567
Enterprise mode uses eight oh two dot one X with a radius server.

00:03:34.567 --> 00:03:42.960
The S S I D is the network name, the B S S I D identifies one radio, and the E S S I D is one network across many access points.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:46.501
Band steering moves capable clients to five gigahertz.

00:03:46.501 --> 00:03:49.189
Site surveys and heat maps plan coverage.

00:03:49.189 --> 00:03:53.582
And lightweight access points are managed by a wireless controller.

00:03:54.282 --> 00:03:55.420
Antennas and modes.

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:58.176
Omnidirectional antennas cover all directions.

00:03:58.176 --> 00:04:02.250
Directional antennas focus the signal for long point-to-point links.

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:06.084
In infrastructure mode, clients connect through an access point.

00:04:06.084 --> 00:04:08.540
In ad hoc mode, devices connect directly.

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:11.536
In a mesh, access points relay traffic wirelessly.

00:04:11.536 --> 00:04:15.969
And remember that wider channels are faster but pick up more interference.

00:04:16.669 --> 00:04:18.581
Finally, physical installation.

00:04:18.581 --> 00:04:21.728
A rack unit, one U, is one point seven five inches.

00:04:21.728 --> 00:04:26.539
Cables terminate on patch panels and on one-ten or sixty-six punchdown blocks.

00:04:26.539 --> 00:04:31.412
The M D F is the main distribution closet, and an I D F is a closet on a floor.

00:04:31.412 --> 00:04:34.250
Use plenum-rated cable in air-handling spaces.

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:37.705
Arrange racks in hot aisles and cold aisles for cooling.

00:04:37.705 --> 00:04:42.023
Provide U P S and P D U power, with redundancy, and label every cable.

00:04:42.023 --> 00:04:45.415
A fiber distribution panel organizes fiber connections.

00:04:46.115 --> 00:04:57.197
To recap domain two: remember the route selection order, know each protocol and its type, understand access and trunk ports and the native V LAN, and know the spanning tree roles.

00:04:57.197 --> 00:05:02.459
Know the P o E levels and the Wi-Fi standards, and plan power, cooling, and labeling.

00:05:02.459 --> 00:05:03.078
Good luck.
